      <div class="research-entry-content">
        <h3>
          <a href="/projects/uot-detr">
            Distributionally Constrained Visual Quality Measurement Using
            Unbalanced Optimal Transport: Wood-Chip Size Estimation for
            Manufacturing Process Monitoring
          </a>
        </h3>

        <p class="research-entry-authors">
          <strong>Amirhossein Eskorouchi</strong>, Jason T. Street,
          Haifeng Wang, and Mohammad Marufuzzaman
        </p>

        <p class="research-entry-status">
          Under review at <em>Decision Support Systems</em>
        </p>

        <p class="research-entry-links" aria-label="UOT-DETR resources">
          <a
            href="https://github.com/amirhossein-eskorouchi/UOT-DETR"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >
            Code
          </a>
          <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
          <a
            href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18392693"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >
            Dataset
          </a>
          <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
          <a href="/projects/uot-detr">Project page</a>
        </p>

        <div class="research-entry-details">
          <p>
            <strong>Description:</strong>{" "}
            Conventional object detectors optimize individual detections but
            may fail to recover the physical size distribution needed for
            manufacturing quality decisions. UOT-DETR addresses this problem by
            adding an unbalanced optimal transport regularizer to DETR training,
            encouraging the predicted wood-chip population to preserve the
            reference size distribution while retaining the standard DETR
            inference process.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Outcome:</strong>{" "}
            UOT-DETR achieved an mAR50 of 95, an overall mAR50-95 of 72, and a
            small-object mAR50-95 of 42 on dense real-world wood-chip imagery.
            Across ten paired experimental seeds, it improved small-object
            mAR50 by 24.7 percentage points and small-object mAP50 by 15.4
            percentage points over DETR, with the largest gains occurring in
            medium- and high-clutter scenes. The training-time regularizer
            introduces no inference-time overhead.
          </p>

          <p class="research-entry-support">
            <strong>Project support:</strong>{" "}USDA-NIFA
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
